          "type": "turn",
          "question": "Do bodies disagree about using a short-acting reliever on its own in asthma?",
          "answer": "Current guidelines emphasize that using a short-acting bronchodilator alone for asthma management is no longer appropriate at any severity. Instead, all adults and adolescents with asthma should receive inhaled corticosteroid-containing therapy. This is because relying solely on a reliever does not address the underlying inflammation and can lead to severe exacerbations, even in those with infrequent symptoms (source: Global respiratory strategy report and national asthma panel guidance).\n\nThere is some disagreement among health authorities regarding the ideal reliever for mild asthma. The global strategy recommends a combination of inhaled corticosteroid and formoterol as the preferred reliever. However, some national guidelines maintain that a short-acting bronchodilator can still be appropriate as an additional reliever alongside a regular controller in mild cases (source: Global respiratory strategy report and national asthma panel guidance).\n\nIn summary, the consensus is clear that short-acting relievers should not be used exclusively. Still, opinions differ on the appropriateness of their use in conjunction with other medications, particularly for those with mild asthma.\n\nNot medical advice. This is an educational software project. It does not diagnose or treat, and it must not be used for real medical decisions.",
